       (could not be fetched from the assistant's sandbox; download manually)
       Metrics/tables needed:  (describe)

Design implication: the system manifest should carry a "section" (or test-set)
column so one pipeline can serve all sections, and tables can be
generated per section.  Systems can be added to any section independently.
Suggested order of work: start Phase 0/3 with the macrocycle and covalent
minimization scripts (sections 1 and 2/2a), then the remaining sections.


REPO SURVEY: teb_docking_test_sets  (read-only look, 2026-11-24)
----------------------------------------------------------------
Repo: git@github.com:tbalius/teb_docking_test_sets.git
Branches: main, version1, version2, and work branch
          dock614_retrospective_testing_with_Claude
Size: about 4,000 files (mostly mol2/pdb; ~95 csh, ~11 Python scripts).

  RAS_pub_testset/files_for_docking/   ready-to-dock inputs, one directory
      per system named PDBID_LIG: 140 RAS (KRAS/HRAS/NRAS/DNA), 43 DUDE-Z,
      207 covalent (Scarpino et al.).  Each has rec_complete / lig_complete
      mol2+pdb; covalent ones add cov_sph.pdb and covlig_complete*.mol2.
  RAS_pub_testset/process_prepare_pdb_files_scripts/   0000-0009 numbered
      csh/py prep scripts (Chimera, antechamber; covalent and non-covalent).
  RAS_pub_testset/dock_setups_scripts/   0001-0004 spheres/grids/
      blastermaster; GIST and 3D-RISM water-map pipelines.
  RAS_pub_testset/DUDE-Z-like_ligands_and_decoys/   decoy pipeline + dbs.
  scripts_for_tutorial/   three worked examples (incl. covalent 6OIM).

Observations relevant to the automation:
  - Numbered scripts (0001...) already define stages.
  - Scripts skip a system if its output directory exists (crude "new only"
    mode; a failed/half-finished directory is also skipped).
  - 53 of 62 csh/py scripts contain hard-coded /home/baliuste paths.
  - Scripts write SLURM scripts via csh heredocs, then call sbatch.
  - System list is read from an external file (pdb_lig_map.txt, "PDB LIG"
    pairs) outside the repo.
  - Scripts that run docking and compute tables are NOT in the repo.


PROPOSED LAYOUT  (to be adjusted once existing scripts are in place)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
  README.Roadmap          this file
  setup.sh                sourceable env vars (DOCK_HOME, paths, ...)
  settings.txt            key = value settings (thresholds, versions, ...)
  systems.txt             manifest: one system per line (pdb, ligand, subset)
  scripts/                Python scripts and the csh SLURM scripts
  testset/                per-system input data
  results/<dock_version>/ outputs, one tree per DOCK version
  tables/                 generated tables for the paper


PHASES
------

Phase 0 -- Inventory
  [x] Repo teb_docking_test_sets is in this directory (looked at read-only
      on 2026-11-24; see REPO SURVEY below).
  [x] Work branch created: dock614_retrospective_testing_with_Claude
      (confirm it is checked out before any edits to the repo).
  [ ] Get the scripts that RUN docking and generate tables.  They are not in
      the repo (deliberately -- very hands-on).  Decide where the new
      automated versions will live (repo branch?) and whether the old ones
      are copied in as reference.
  [ ] Commit the macrocycle test set (Y. S. Tan, >100 systems) to the branch.
  [ ] Update teb_docking_test_sets/README.md (see Phase 6 for the proposal).
  [ ] For each script: write a short summary (inputs, outputs, side effects,
      assumptions, where it needs hand-holding).
  [ ] Collect a few example output files from each stage.
  [ ] Decide the list of environment variables (paths only, or also modules,
      partition, account?).

Phase 1 -- Setup and configuration
  [ ] setup.sh (bash), generated/updated by a setup step, editable by hand.
  [ ] How csh/SLURM scripts get the variables: sbatch --export from Python
      (preferred, single source of truth) or a small setup.csh companion.
  [ ] settings.txt reader (standard library only).
  [ ] Startup checks: required variables set, paths exist, clear messages.

Phase 2 -- System manifest and status tracking
  [ ] systems.txt format and reader.
  [ ] Per-system, per-stage status inferred from expected output files
      (prep, dock, analyze).  Report done / missing / failed.
  [ ] Command-line run modes:
        --new-only        run only systems/stages with missing outputs
                          (default)
        --systems A B     run named systems
        --full            rerun everything
        --stage NAME      restrict to one stage
        --dry-run         show what would run; submit nothing
  [ ] Decide how to handle stale results after a code change
      (results directory per DOCK version is the leading option).

Phase 3 -- Script translation (csh -> Python), one script at a time
  Per script:  summary -> approval -> Python version -> side-by-side check on
  a small case -> move on.
  [ ] (list scripts here once inventoried)
  [ ] Python wrapper for SLURM submission: build sbatch calls, capture job
      IDs, chain stages with --dependency=afterok, check outputs on
      completion, report failures so only those can be resubmitted.

Phase 4 -- Adding new systems
  [ ] Helper to append a row to systems.txt and fetch/prepare inputs.
  [ ] Automate mechanical prep steps; flag steps needing human review
      (protonation, cofactors, waters, etc.).
  [ ] Run on new systems only, then regenerate tables.

Phase 5 -- Statistics and tables for the paper
  [ ] Parsers for DOCK outputs (scores, RMSD, etc.).
  [ ] Metrics (to be confirmed): redocking success rate at a given RMSD
      threshold, enrichment (ROC/AUC, EF), breakdown by subset.
  [ ] Tables written as tab-delimited/CSV text first; convert to LaTeX or
      Word/Excel to match the journal template.
  [ ] Tables built from ALL available results; list missing or failed systems
      so nothing is silently excluded.
  [ ] One command regenerates every number and table in the paper.

Phase 6 -- Cleanup and reproducibility
  [ ] Update teb_docking_test_sets/README.md (currently only a title and
      clone instructions).  Proposed contents: purpose (docking test sets
      and setup scripts from the RAS Comp Chem team, for retrospective
      testing of DOCK 6.14); map of the directories; what each branch is
      for (main, version1, version2, and the work branch); status and plans
      with a pointer to README.Roadmap; conventions (plain-text manifests
      and settings, no hard-coded paths, DOCK version recorded with
      results); requirements (DOCK 6.14, Chimera, AmberTools, SLURM -- to
      confirm).  Decide whether README.Roadmap moves into the repo so it is
      versioned on the work branch.
  [ ] Document usage in a README (how to add a system, rerun, regenerate).
  [ ] Compare results between DOCK versions if desired.
  [ ] Final verification: spot-check tables against raw outputs.


OPEN QUESTIONS
--------------
- Which standard test set(s): SB2010, SB2025, DT1244, DT100, or several?
- DT1244 vs SB2025: same systems, different preparation.  Is the paper
  comparing the two preparations, or just picking one?
- Is DT100 a subset of DT1244, and is it a random or curated selection?
  (Proposed use: fast development/regression set.)
- For each of the four paper sections: what is the test set, which scripts
  run it, and what does success look like (RMSD, enrichment, built-structure
  quality, crosslink geometry, ...)?
- Do the four sections share one pipeline, or does each need its own?
- When is DOCK 6.14 expected, and what build do we develop against until then
  (dev branch / release candidate)?
- Which environment variables belong in the setup file?
- Which metrics and tables does the paper need (rough sketch is fine)?
- Where are the scripts that run docking and generate tables (cluster or
  local)?  Do the macrocycle and covalent minimization scripts live in the
  repo or elsewhere?
- What do the per-system directories look like in the existing test set?
- Where is DOCK 6 run (cluster name, modules, SLURM partition/account)?
- Python version available on the cluster?


